# Cursor Rules — LLM Prompt Injection Scanner
# Rod's AI Security Portfolio Project

## Who I am
I am a self-taught developer transitioning into AI security engineering.
I am building this as a portfolio piece for roles at companies like Google and Anthropic.
I have a background in hardware/IT support and I am actively learning Python and cloud security.

## What this project is
A command-line tool that tests LLM APIs (starting with Anthropic Claude) for prompt injection
vulnerabilities. It sends structured attack payloads, analyzes responses, scores risk levels,
and generates an HTML report. Based on OWASP LLM Top 10.

## Stack
- Python 3.11+
- anthropic SDK (official Python client)
- pytest for unit tests
- No external frameworks — stdlib + anthropic only

## Code style rules
- PEP8 always
- Type hints on every function signature
- Docstrings on every function — explain WHAT it does AND WHY it matters for security
- Descriptive variable names — no single letters except loop counters
- Keep functions small (under 30 lines ideally) — one job per function
- Use dataclasses for structured data (ScanResult, Payload, Report)
- Constants in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE at the top of each module

## Security-specific rules
- Never hardcode API keys — always os.environ.get()
- Add a comment explaining the security concept behind each payload category
- When writing detection logic, explain the indicator being detected and why it signals injection
- Reference OWASP LLM Top 10 categories in comments where relevant (LLM01, LLM02, etc.)

## Learning mode
I am learning as I build. When I ask you to explain code, teach the security concept
behind it — not just the syntax. Connect the code to real-world attack scenarios.
If I write something that could be improved for security or readability, tell me why.

## What NOT to do
- Do not use pandas, numpy, or heavy data libs — keep dependencies minimal
- Do not generate UI frameworks — this is a CLI tool with HTML report output only
- Do not skip docstrings or type hints to save space
- Do not write functions longer than 40 lines without asking me first
